    "q-160892": {
      "dom_id": "step-q160892",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},\\,v=xy,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3},\\,y=\\frac{2}{x},\\,y=\\frac{4}{x}.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y={x}^{2} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}=1 & ⟹\\, & u=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{2}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=2 & ⟹\\, & v=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{4}{x} & ⟹\\, & xy=4 & ⟹\\, & v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14169255/assets/1207d8cdb8afd7c55d712caca043edfe9f0d0395343a6cbd329cf5861ec3bce7.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{y} & -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{3{x}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-160892-a",
